## 2. Shadow pairs

| A | B | Overlap reason | Who wins |
|---|---|---|---|
| x86_match_list_test (x86_emit_list.oo:83) | x86_match_list (x86_emit_list.oo:28) | list_test is a 3-line wrapper that returns x86_match_list(src). Dispatch at x86_emit.oo:167 calls list_test first; the second arm at line 172 calls x86_match_list. The second arm is unreachable when list_test is non-empty, and they produce the same result. | list_test at x86_emit.oo:167 |
| x86_emit_float.oo (x86_collect_and_emit_float) | x86_ssa_emit_float.oo (x86_ssa_emit_float_program) | Both do const-fold `let [mut] n: Float = <chain>; println(<chain>);` with fixed-point scale 1e6. parse_v/fmt_v/op_v/fold_v are duplicated verbatim. The _println_float body is also duplicated. | typed-AST first at x86_emit.oo:74; float_test is fallback at line 192 |
| x86_emit_match.oo (x86_match_match_test) | x86_ssa_emit.oo (x86_ssa_emit_match_program) | Both lower match { 2..8 arms } on tagged union [tag,payload] to cmpq/je cascade. Token-level vs char-walk; same output structure. | typed-AST first at x86_emit.oo:74 |
| x86_emit_list.oo (x86_match_list) | x86_ssa_emit.oo (x86_ssa_emit_list_program) | Both walk tokens for list_new/list_push(IDENT,INT)/list_get/list_set/list_len and emit the same SysV calls. | typed-AST first at x86_emit.oo:74; token-level fallback at line 167 |
| x86_emit_int.oo (x86_emit_int_routine) | x86_ssa_emit.oo (x86_ssa_emit_println_int) | Same _println_int routine body: movb $10 stack buffer, div-by-10 loop, sys_write. One as print statements, one as returned string. | both used (print vs string) |
| x86_emit_float_routine.oo (x86_emit_float_routine) | x86_ssa_emit_float.oo (x86_ssa_emit_float_routine_text) | Same _println_float body; one as print, one as returned string. | both used (print vs string) |
| x86_emit_free.oo (x86_emit_free_routines) | x86_emit_heap.oo (x86_emit_heap_allocator) | Both define oo_x86_alloc (different bodies: freelist vs mmap). A third copy lives in x86_runtime.oo:30-40. | by token match: free at x86_emit.oo:182 beats heap at line 199 |
| x86_emit_fn.oo (x86_match_id_call) | x86_emit_greet.oo (x86_match_greet_call) | Both require 1-arg main body `println(NAME(STRING))` and a 1-String-arg fn. Return shape diverges: fn wants `return p` (no PLUS); greet wants `return STRING + p + STRING` (with PLUS). | id_call at x86_emit.oo:88 |
| x86_emit_rsi.oo (x86_match_snd_call) | x86_emit_str.oo (x86_match_cat_call) | Both require 2-arg main body `println(NAME(STRING, STRING))` and a 2-String-arg fn. Return shape diverges: snd wants `return p2`; cat wants `return p1 + p2`. | snd_call at x86_emit.oo:92 |
| x86_emit_fold.oo (x86_fold_hello) | x86_emit_greet.oo (x86_match_greet_call) | Both match the 1-arg `fn(p) { return STRING + p + STRING; }` body. fold is restricted to fn name "greet" and RBRACE-tight position; greet accepts any name. | fold at x86_emit.oo:80 |
| x86_emit_typed_let.oo (x86_match_typed_let) | x86_emit_walk.oo (x86_collect_prints) | typed_let matches `let n: Int = V; println(n)` and emits stack-slot load. walk handles the same shape via x86_apply_let and emits inline-const syscalls. | typed_let at x86_emit.oo:137 |
| x86_emit_rdx.oo + x86_emit_rcx.oo + x86_emit_r89.oo | (within rdx/rcx/r89 family) | All three match the "return last String param" shape at 3-arg, 4-arg, and 5/6-arg respectively. r89 has a generic x86_match_n_call(src, k_params) that rdx/rcx could be rewritten over. | per arg count in dispatch order |

## 3. KEEP BOTH recommendations

1. fn vs greet (1-arg): divergent return shape (passthrough vs concat). KEEP BOTH.
2. rsi vs str (2-arg): divergent return shape (return p2 vs return p1+p2). KEEP BOTH.
3. rdx/rcx/r89 family: each occupies a distinct arg-count slot in the dispatch ladder; consolidating would change the dispatch order. KEEP BOTH; consider a follow-up to route 3-arg and 4-arg through x86_match_n_call.
4. fold vs greet: fold is a const-fold path for hello.oo (RBRACE-tight); greet is the runtime copy. Different output strategies. KEEP BOTH.
5. int vs typed_let: int matches `println(INT)`; typed_let matches `let n: Int = V; println(n)`. Distinct body shapes. KEEP BOTH.
6. typed_let vs walk: typed_let emits stack-slot load; walk emits inline-const syscall. KEEP BOTH; typed_let wins first.
7. free vs heap: free matches `oo_alloc`/`heap_free_test`; heap matches `heap_alloc_test`. Distinct identifier sets. KEEP BOTH matchers; consolidate the oo_x86_alloc body (see MERGE #5).
8. secret vs contract: orthogonal pre-emit gates (taint vs const-fold). KEEP BOTH.

## 4. MERGE recommendations

1. Float const-fold: extract parse_v/fmt_v/op_v/fold_v into a shared `x86_float_fold.oo` (new). Have x86_emit_float.oo and x86_ssa_emit_float.oo import it. The 129-line x86_ssa_emit_float.oo shrinks to ~30 lines.
2. _println_float routine: have x86_emit_float_routine.oo return the verbatim body string (instead of printing). x86_emit_float.oo and x86_ssa_emit_float.oo then call the same fn. The 85-line x86_emit_float_routine.oo body becomes a single return.
3. _println_int routine: have x86_emit_int.oo's x86_emit_int_routine return the body string. x86_ssa_emit_println_int then calls it. Remove the 30-line duplicate body in x86_ssa_emit.oo:94-97.
4. List source-level pattern: extract `x86_match_list_ops(src)` from x86_emit_list.oo into a shared helper; have x86_ssa_emit_list_program import it instead of re-walking the same tokens.
5. oo_x86_alloc: keep one definition (x86_runtime.oo). Delete the oo_x86_alloc body in x86_emit_heap.oo:18-50 and the oo_x86_alloc body inside x86_emit_free.oo:38-51; have both modules import x86_runtime_text() instead.
6. x86_ssa_emit_match_program: extract the match-arm walker into a shared `x86_match_arms.oo`; have x86_emit_match.oo and x86_ssa_emit.oo use the same arm-parser instead of two divergent walkers.

## 5. DELETE recommendations

1. DELETE: the second list dispatch arm at x86_emit.oo:172-176 (the `let lp2: List[String] = x86_match_list(src); if list_len(lp2) >= 2 { x86_emit_list_program(src); return; }` block). Reason: x86_emit.oo:167-171 already calls x86_match_list_test which is a 3-line wrapper for x86_match_list; the second arm is unreachable for the same source and a strict duplicate. Net: 5 source lines in x86_emit.oo removed.
2. DELETE: x86_match_list_test wrapper at x86_emit_list.oo:83-85. The wrapper adds no value; inline x86_match_list into the dispatch arm at x86_emit.oo:167. Net: 3 lines in x86_emit_list.oo removed.
3. DELETE: x86_emit_list_test_run at x86_emit_list.oo:87-90. Reason: it passes empty src to x86_emit_list_program, which then ERRs. With #1 and #2 applied, the dispatch calls x86_emit_list_program(src) directly with the original src, making this 4-line broken stub dead. Net: 4 lines removed; required for the list path to actually compile a real list source.

## Totals

- Modules audited: 38 feature emit modules (35 x86_emit_*.oo + 3 x86_ssa_emit_*.oo; the main dispatchers x86_emit.oo and x86_ssa_emit.oo are excluded as they orchestrate, not handle, features; the brief's "41+" includes them plus x86_runtime.oo, x86_ast_typed.oo, and x86_ssa_compile.oo, bringing the backend total to 42 files)
- Shadow pairs: 12
- KEEP BOTH recommendations: 8
- MERGE recommendations: 6
- DELETE recommendations: 3
- Lines saved by all DELETEs: 12 (5 in x86_emit.oo + 3 in x86_emit_list.oo from #2 + 4 in x86_emit_list.oo from #3)
